// Question library: Xiaodong Yang's quant interview notes (probability, statistics, time series,
// derivatives, Greeks, stochastic calculus, machine learning and coding), rewritten in our own
// words with our own hints and answers. Definition questions are open-ended with a model answer;
// where a formula can be tried on numbers we ask for the number. Exact repeats of puzzles already in
// the library (Monty Hall, the drunk passenger, the uniform sum, burning ropes) are left out.
// Numeric answers are re-derived in research/verify_library.py.
(function () {
  const EXACT = { abs: 0.5, rel: 0 };
  // Open question with a model answer: [id, category, level, title, question, answer html, hints].
  const O = (id, cat, lvl, title, q, model, h) => [id, cat, lvl, title, q, { open: `<p>${model}</p>`, h }];

  QT.library.add('xy', [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- probability
    O('bayes-rule', 'Bayes', 'e', "Bayes' rule", `State Bayes' rule and say why it comes up so often in quant interviews.`,
      `P(A | B) = P(B | A) P(A) / P(B), where P(B) = Σ P(B | Aᵢ) P(Aᵢ) over the possible causes. It turns "how likely is the evidence under each hypothesis" into "how likely is each hypothesis given the evidence". Interviews use it for medical-test and signal questions, updating a view after a trade, and spotting base-rate mistakes: a rare cause stays fairly unlikely even after strong evidence.`,
      [`Start from the definition P(A ∩ B) = P(A | B) P(B) = P(B | A) P(A).`, `Think of a disease test: what matters besides the test's accuracy?`]),
    ['first-head', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Waiting for the first head', `You toss a fair coin until the first head.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of tosses?`, a: 2, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`The count is geometric with success probability ½.`, `Condition on the first toss: E = 1 + ½ × 0 + ½ × E.`],
        s: [`Geometric waiting time: E = 1/p.`, [`1/(½) = <b>2</b>.`], `Half the time it takes one toss; the other half you've wasted one and start again.`, ``] }]],
    ['max-uniform', 'Probability', 'm', 'The largest of four uniforms', `U₁, …, U₄ are independent uniform on [0, 1].`,
      [{ q: `What is E[max(U₁, …, U₄)]?`, a: 0.8,
        h: [`P(max ≤ x) = x⁴, since all four must be ≤ x.`, `Differentiate to get the density 4x³, or use E[M] = ∫ P(M > x) dx.`, `In general E[max of n] = n/(n + 1).`],
        s: [`The max of n uniforms has CDF xⁿ and mean n/(n + 1).`, [`E = ∫₀¹ (1 − x⁴) dx = 1 − 1/5.`, `= <b>4/5 = 0.8</b>.`], `By symmetry the four points split [0, 1] into five gaps of average 1/5, and the max sits one gap from the top.`, ``] }]],
    ['poisson-even', 'Probability', 'm', 'A Poisson count being even', `N has a Poisson distribution with mean 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is P(N is even)?`, a: 0.5 * (1 + Math.exp(-2)), tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`P(even) − P(odd) = Σ (−1)ⁿ e<sup>−λ</sup> λⁿ/n!.`, `That sum is e<sup>−λ</sup> e<sup>−λ</sup> = e<sup>−2λ</sup>.`, `Combine with P(even) + P(odd) = 1.`],
        s: [`Use the generating-function trick with z = −1.`, [`P(even) − P(odd) = e<sup>−2</sup>.`, `P(even) = (1 + e<sup>−2</sup>)/2 ≈ <b>0.568</b>.`], `It's always above ½, approaching ½ as λ grows.`, ``] }]],
    ['ruin-fair', 'Random walks', 'e', 'Symmetric ruin', `A fair ±1 random walk starts at 3 and stops at 0 or 10.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability it reaches 10 first?`, a: 0.3,
        h: [`The walk is a martingale, so its expected final position is its start.`, `Final position is 10 with probability p and 0 otherwise.`],
        s: [`Optional stopping: 10p = 3.`, [`p = 3/10 = <b>0.3</b>.`], `In general i/b.`, ``] }]],
    O('axioms', 'Probability', 'e', 'The probability axioms', `What are the axioms of probability, and how do they help with interview problems?`,
      `(1) P(A) ≥ 0 for every event; (2) P(Ω) = 1; (3) for disjoint events, P(⋃Aᵢ) = Σ P(Aᵢ) (countably many). Everything else follows, for example P(Aᶜ) = 1 − P(A) and inclusion–exclusion. In practice they justify the two moves you use most: split an event into disjoint cases and add, or compute the complement.`,
      [`There are three: one about sign, one about the whole space, one about disjoint unions.`, `Which everyday rules (complements, adding cases) follow from them?`]),
    O('conditional', 'Conditional probability', 'e', 'Conditional probability', `Define conditional probability.`,
      `P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) for P(B) > 0: the chance of A once you know B happened, i.e. restrict attention to the outcomes in B and rescale so they add to 1. Multiplying out gives the chain rule P(A ∩ B) = P(A | B) P(B).`,
      [`Knowing B shrinks the set of possible outcomes.`, `Within B, what fraction of the probability also lies in A?`]),
    O('total-prob', 'Conditional probability', 'e', 'The law of total probability', `State the law of total probability and when you'd use it.`,
      `If A₁, …, Aₙ partition the outcomes, P(B) = Σ P(B | Aᵢ) P(Aᵢ). Use it when B is easy to work out once you know which hidden case you're in (which coin was picked, which state the market is in), and as the denominator in Bayes' rule.`,
      [`Split the world into cases that can't overlap and cover everything.`, `Within each case, B's probability is easy.`]),
    ['bernoulli-var', 'Statistics', 'e', 'A Bernoulli variance', `X is 1 with probability 0.3 and 0 otherwise.`,
      [{ q: `What is Var(X)?`, a: 0.21,
        h: [`E[X] = p and, since X² = X, E[X²] = p too.`, `Var = E[X²] − E[X]².`],
        s: [`Var = p(1 − p).`, [`0.3 × 0.7 = <b>0.21</b>.`], `The variance is largest, ¼, at p = ½.`, ``] }]],
    ['binomial-moments', 'Statistics', 'e', 'Binomial mean and variance', `X counts successes in 20 independent trials, each succeeding with probability 0.3.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is E[X]?`, a: 6, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`X is a sum of 20 Bernoulli(0.3) indicators.`, `Expectations add.`],
        s: [`Linearity: np.`, [`20 × 0.3 = <b>6</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is Var(X)?`, a: 4.2,
        h: [`The trials are independent, so variances add.`, `Each indicator has variance p(1 − p).`],
        s: [`Independent variances add: np(1 − p).`, [`20 × 0.3 × 0.7 = <b>4.2</b>.`], `So the standard deviation is about 2.05.`, ``] }]],
    O('poisson-uses', 'Probability', 'e', 'Where Poisson shows up', `When is the Poisson distribution useful in quant finance?`,
      `For counts of rare, independent events in a fixed window: order arrivals, trades per second, defaults in a portfolio, jumps in a price process. N ~ Poisson(λt) has mean and variance both λt, and the gaps between events are exponential. Real order flow is usually over-dispersed (variance above the mean, events clustering), which is a common follow-up.`,
      [`Think of counting events that happen independently at a steady rate.`, `What are its mean and variance, and is real market data like that?`]),
    O('linearity', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Linearity of expectation', `Why is linearity of expectation so useful in interviews?`,
      `E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y] holds whether or not X and Y are independent. So you can write a messy count as a sum of indicator variables, find each indicator's probability (often by symmetry), and add, without ever working out the joint distribution. Fixed points of a permutation, empty bins, runs in a sequence, and expected matches all fall to this.`,
      [`Does E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y] need independence?`, `How do indicator variables turn a count into a sum?`]),
    O('var-cov-corr', 'Statistics', 'e', 'Variance, covariance, correlation', `How are variance, covariance and correlation related?`,
      `Var(X) = E[X²] − E[X]² = Cov(X, X). Cov(X, Y) = E[XY] − E[X]E[Y] measures linear co-movement in the variables' units. Correlation rescales it to [−1, 1]: ρ = Cov(X, Y)/(σ_X σ_Y). Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2Cov(X, Y). Zero correlation doesn't imply independence (e.g. X and X² for symmetric X).`,
      [`Write each as an expectation.`, `What does correlation add that covariance lacks?`]),
    ['ruin-unfair', 'Random walks', 'm', 'Ruin with a slight edge against you', `A walk moves up with probability 0.45 and down with 0.55, starting at 10 and stopping at 0 or 20.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability it reaches 20 first?`, a: (1 - (0.55 / 0.45) ** 10) / (1 - (0.55 / 0.45) ** 20),
        h: [`With r = q/p, P(hit b first from i) = (1 − rⁱ)/(1 − rᵇ).`, `Here r = 0.55/0.45 ≈ 1.222.`],
        s: [`Gambler's ruin with a bias.`, [`r¹⁰ ≈ 7.43, r²⁰ ≈ 55.2.`, `P ≈ (1 − 7.43)/(1 − 55.2) ≈ <b>0.119</b>.`], `A fair game would give 0.5: a 5% edge against you over a long game is crushing.`, ``] }]],
    O('markov-property', 'Markov chains', 'e', 'The Markov property', `What is the Markov property?`,
      `The future depends on the past only through the present: P(X<sub>n+1</sub> = x | X<sub>n</sub>, …, X₀) = P(X<sub>n+1</sub> = x | X<sub>n</sub>). So a process is described by its current state and transition probabilities. Random walks, credit-rating transitions and many queueing models are Markov; you can often make a process Markov by adding to the state (e.g. "last two tosses").`,
      [`What information about the past does the next step need?`, `If a process isn't Markov, how could you enlarge the state to make it so?`]),
    O('stopping-time', 'Random walks', 'm', 'Stopping times', `What is a stopping time, intuitively?`,
      `A random time τ such that, at any moment, you can tell from the information so far whether it has already happened: {τ ≤ t} is known at time t. "The first time the walk hits 10" is a stopping time; "the time the walk reaches its maximum" isn't, because you'd need the future. Optional stopping (E[M<sub>τ</sub>] = M₀ for a martingale, under conditions) works with stopping times.`,
      [`Could you decide to stop using only what you've seen so far?`, `Compare "first time above 10" with "the time of the overall maximum".`]),
    O('useful-info', 'Conditional probability', 'm', 'When information matters', `In conditional-probability puzzles, how do you tell useful information from useless information?`,
      `Ask <b>how the information was produced</b>. If the way it was revealed depends on the hidden outcome (Monty knowingly opens a goat door; you were told "at least one boy" rather than meeting a random child), it changes the probabilities. If it would have been revealed the same way whatever the hidden state, it doesn't. Write down the likelihood of the observation under each hypothesis; if those are equal, the information is useless.`,
      [`Would you have been told the same thing whatever the hidden truth?`, `Compare P(observation | each hypothesis).`]),
    ['meet-15', 'Probability', 'e', 'Meeting with 15-minute waits', `Two friends arrive independently and uniformly within an hour; each waits 15 minutes for the other.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability they meet?`, a: 7 / 16,
        h: [`They meet if their arrival times differ by at most ¼ hour.`, `Draw the unit square; the misses are two corner triangles with legs ¾.`],
        s: [`Area of |x − y| ≤ ¼ in the unit square.`, [`Misses: 2 × ½ × (¾)² = 9/16.`, `P = 1 − 9/16 = <b>7/16 ≈ 0.4375</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['meet-unequal', 'Probability', 'h', 'Meeting with unequal waits', `A arrives uniformly within an hour and waits 15 minutes; B arrives uniformly within the same hour and waits 30 minutes.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability they meet?`, a: 1 - ((0.75 ** 2) + (0.5 ** 2)) / 2,
        h: [`They meet if B arrives within A's 15 minutes after A, or A arrives within B's 30 minutes after B.`, `So they miss only if B comes more than ¼ hour after A, or A more than ½ hour after B.`, `The two miss triangles have legs ¾ and ½.`],
        s: [`Two corner triangles of different sizes.`, [`Misses: ½(¾)² + ½(½)² = 0.28125 + 0.125.`, `P = 1 − 0.40625 = <b>0.59375</b>.`], `With both waiting 15 minutes it's 0.4375; B's longer wait helps.`, ``] }]],
    ['birthday-23', 'Probability', 'm', 'The birthday problem', `23 people, birthdays independent and uniform over 365 days.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability at least two share a birthday?`, a: 1 - Array.from({ length: 23 }, (_, i) => (365 - i) / 365).reduce((p, x) => p * x, 1), tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Compute the complement: all birthdays different.`, `P(all different) = (365/365)(364/365)…(343/365).`, `ln P ≈ −Σ i/365 = −(22 × 23/2)/365.`],
        s: [`Complement of "all different".`, [`P(all different) ≈ e<sup>−253/365</sup> ≈ 0.493.`, `P(shared) ≈ <b>0.507</b>.`], `23 people make 253 pairs, each matching with probability 1/365.`, ``] }]],
    ['empty-bins', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Empty bins', `10 balls are thrown independently into 10 bins.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of empty bins?`, a: 10 * 0.9 ** 10, tol: { abs: 0.005, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Use an indicator for each bin being empty.`, `A given bin misses all 10 balls with probability (9/10)¹⁰.`],
        s: [`Linearity over the bins.`, [`10 × 0.9¹⁰ ≈ 10 × 0.349 = <b>3.49</b>.`], `For large n, about n/e of the bins stay empty.`, ``] }]],
    ['gamma-moments', 'Statistics', 'm', 'Gamma mean and variance', `X has a Gamma distribution with shape k = 3 and scale θ = 2 (a sum of 3 independent exponentials with mean 2).`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is E[X]?`, a: 6, tol: EXACT, h: [`It's a sum of 3 exponentials with mean 2.`, `Means add.`],
        s: [`E = kθ.`, [`3 × 2 = <b>6</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is Var(X)?`, a: 12, tol: EXACT, h: [`Each exponential with mean 2 has variance 4.`, `They're independent, so variances add.`],
        s: [`Var = kθ².`, [`3 × 4 = <b>12</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['die-11', 'Expected value', 'h', 'Waiting for two 1s in a row', `You roll a fair die repeatedly.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of rolls until two 1s in a row?`, a: 42, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Let E₀ be from scratch and E₁ after rolling a 1.`, `E₀ = 1 + (1/6)E₁ + (5/6)E₀ and E₁ = 1 + (5/6)E₀.`, `Solve the two equations.`],
        s: [`Two-state recursion; missing the second 1 sends you back to the start.`, [`E₀ = 6 + E₁ and E₁ = 1 + (5/6)E₀.`, `E₀ = 7 + (5/6)E₀ ⇒ E₀ = <b>42</b>.`], `Overlapping patterns take longer: 1-then-2 takes only 36.`, ``] }]],
    ['max-two-uniform', 'Probability', 'e', 'The larger of two uniforms', `U₁ and U₂ are independent uniform on [0, 1].`,
      [{ q: `What is P(max(U₁, U₂) > 0.8)?`, a: 0.36,
        h: [`P(max ≤ 0.8) = P(both ≤ 0.8).`, `Take the complement.`],
        s: [`Complement of both being small.`, [`1 − 0.8² = <b>0.36</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['two-white', 'Probability', 'e', 'Two white balls', `A box holds 3 white and 2 black balls. Two are drawn without replacement.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability both are white?`, a: 0.3,
        h: [`First white: 3/5. Then a white from what's left.`, `Multiply.`],
        s: [`Chain rule.`, [`3/5 × 2/4 = <b>0.3</b>.`], `Counting: C(3, 2)/C(5, 2) = 3/10.`, ``] }]],
    ['exp-residual', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Memoryless waiting', `X is exponential with mean 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is E[X | X > 1]?`, a: 2, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`The exponential distribution is memoryless.`, `Given X > 1, the extra time X − 1 is again exponential with mean 1.`],
        s: [`Memorylessness: E[X | X > 1] = 1 + E[X].`, [`1 + 1 = <b>2</b>.`], ``, `Answering 1, forgetting that you've already waited one unit.`] }]],
    O('total-expectation', 'Expected value', 'e', 'The law of total expectation', `State the law of total expectation and how it's used.`,
      `E[X] = E[E[X | Y]]: average the conditional expectations over the cases. It's the workhorse behind "condition on the first step" recursions (dice games, waiting times, Markov chains) and behind random sums: E[X₁ + … + X<sub>N</sub>] = E[N] E[X] when N is independent of the Xs.`,
      [`Split by the value of something you can condition on.`, `How do first-step recursions use it?`]),
    O('signal-extraction', 'Bayes', 'm', 'Bayesian signal extraction', `Why do Bayesian signal-extraction questions appear in quant interviews?`,
      `Trading is inference: a hidden state (an informed buyer, a regime, a stock's true value) shows up only through noisy signals (order flow, a forecast, an earnings beat). Interviewers want to see you set up a prior, a likelihood for the signal under each state, and update with Bayes, and that you know a weak signal should move you only a little. With normal priors and signals, the posterior mean is a precision-weighted average.`,
      [`What's hidden, and what do you observe?`, `How much should a noisy signal move your estimate?`]),
    O('order-density', 'Statistics', 'h', 'Density of an order statistic', `For n independent draws with CDF F and density f, what is the density of the k-th smallest?`,
      `f<sub>(k)</sub>(x) = n!/((k − 1)!(n − k)!) · F(x)<sup>k−1</sup> · (1 − F(x))<sup>n−k</sup> · f(x). Read it as: choose which draw lands at x (density f), which k − 1 fall below x, and which n − k above. For uniforms the k-th smallest is Beta(k, n − k + 1) with mean k/(n + 1).`,
      [`One draw must land at x; how many below and how many above?`, `Count the ways to choose which draws go where.`]),
    ['uniform-range', 'Probability', 'm', 'Range of four uniforms', `U₁, …, U₄ are independent uniform on [0, 1].`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected range, max − min?`, a: 0.6,
        h: [`E[max] = n/(n + 1) and E[min] = 1/(n + 1).`, `Subtract.`],
        s: [`E[range] = (n − 1)/(n + 1).`, [`4/5 − 1/5 = <b>3/5</b>.`], `The points split [0, 1] into 5 gaps averaging 1/5; the range skips the two end gaps.`, ``] }]],
    ['exp-difference', 'Probability', 'h', 'One exponential beating another by 1', `X and Y are independent exponentials with mean 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is P(X − Y > 1)?`, a: 1 / (2 * Math.E),
        h: [`First P(X > Y) = ½ by symmetry.`, `Given X > Y, the excess X − Y is again exponential with mean 1 (memorylessness).`, `So P(X − Y > 1) = ½ × e<sup>−1</sup>.`],
        s: [`Memorylessness: once X passes Y, the overshoot is a fresh exponential.`, [`½ × e<sup>−1</sup> = 1/(2e) ≈ <b>0.184</b>.`], `Direct integration ∫₁^∞ e<sup>−x</sup>(1 − e<sup>−(x−1)</sup>) dx gives the same.`, ``] }]],
    O('correlated-normals', 'Statistics', 'm', 'Simulating correlated normals', `How do you simulate correlated normal random variables (say, in Python)?`,
      `Take the desired covariance matrix Σ and its <b>Cholesky factor</b> L (Σ = LLᵀ). Draw a vector Z of independent standard normals and set X = μ + LZ; then Cov(X) = L·I·Lᵀ = Σ. For two variables with correlation ρ: X₁ = Z₁, X₂ = ρZ₁ + √(1 − ρ²)Z₂. In NumPy: <code>L = np.linalg.cholesky(S); X = Z @ L.T</code> (or <code>rng.multivariate_normal</code>). Σ must be positive definite.`,
      [`Start from independent normals and mix them linearly.`, `Which matrix square root of Σ is triangular?`, `For two variables, how much of Z₁ should X₂ contain?`]),
    ['third-arrival', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Waiting for the third arrival', `Customers arrive as a Poisson process at 2 per minute.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected time, in minutes, until the third arrival?`, a: 1.5,
        h: [`Gaps between arrivals are exponential with mean 1/λ.`, `The third arrival is the sum of three gaps.`],
        s: [`Sum of three exponential gaps (a Gamma(3) time).`, [`3 × ½ = <b>1.5</b> minutes.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['two-heads', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Waiting for two heads', `A coin lands heads with probability 0.3. You toss until you've seen two heads (not necessarily in a row).`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of tosses?`, a: 2 / 0.3,
        h: [`It's the sum of two independent geometric waits.`, `Each has mean 1/p.`],
        s: [`Negative binomial: r/p.`, [`2/0.3 ≈ <b>6.67</b>.`], `For a fair coin, 4.`, ``] }]],
    ['geometric-var', 'Statistics', 'm', 'Variance of a geometric wait', `N counts the trials up to and including the first success, with success probability ¼.`,
      [{ q: `What is Var(N)?`, a: 12, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`The formula is (1 − p)/p².`, `Or compute E[N²] by conditioning on the first trial.`],
        s: [`Var = (1 − p)/p².`, [`(¾)/(1/16) = <b>12</b>.`], `The mean is 4, so the spread is large: SD ≈ 3.5.`, ``] }]],
    ['exp-capped', 'Expected value', 'm', 'A capped exponential', `X is exponential with mean 1 and c = 2.`,
      [{ q: `What is E[min(X, 2)]?`, a: 1 - Math.exp(-2), tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`E[min(X, c)] = ∫₀<sup>c</sup> P(X > t) dt.`, `P(X > t) = e<sup>−t</sup>.`],
        s: [`Integrate the survival function up to the cap.`, [`∫₀² e<sup>−t</sup> dt = 1 − e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ <b>0.865</b>.`], `It has a point mass e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ 0.135 at 2.`, ``] }]],
    ['min-exponentials', 'Expected value', 'e', 'The first of two exponentials', `X and Y are independent exponentials with mean 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is E[min(X, Y)]?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`P(min > t) = P(X > t) P(Y > t).`, `So the minimum is exponential with rate 2.`],
        s: [`Rates add for the first of independent exponentials.`, [`Rate 2, mean <b>½</b>.`], `Hence E[max] = 1 + 1 − ½ = 1.5.`, ``] }]],
    O('random-chord', 'Probability', 'm', 'Bertrand’s random chord', `Why is "pick a random chord of a circle; what's the chance it's longer than the side of the inscribed equilateral triangle?" a trick question?`,
      `Because "random chord" isn't defined. Pick two random points on the circle: ⅓. Pick a random radius and a random point on it as the chord's midpoint: ½. Pick a random midpoint uniformly in the disc: ¼. Each is a legitimate model and they disagree. The interviewer wants you to ask how the chord is generated before computing.`,
      [`List different ways you could "choose a chord at random".`, `Do they all give the same answer?`]),
    ['square-triangle', 'Probability', 'e', 'Below the diagonal', `A point is uniform in the unit square.`,
      [{ q: `What is P(x + y < 1)?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`The region x + y < 1 is a triangle.`, `Probability equals area.`],
        s: [`Area of a right triangle with legs 1.`, [`½ × 1 × 1 = <b>½</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['five-heads', 'Probability', 'e', 'Exactly five heads', `A fair coin is tossed 10 times.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability of exactly 5 heads?`, a: 252 / 1024,
        h: [`Count the sequences with 5 heads: C(10, 5).`, `Each sequence has probability 2<sup>−10</sup>.`],
        s: [`Binomial probability.`, [`C(10, 5) = 252, so 252/1024 ≈ <b>0.246</b>.`], ``, `Expecting about ½ because 5 is the average.`] }]],
    O('mom-mle', 'Statistics', 'e', 'Method of moments v maximum likelihood', `What's the difference between the method of moments and maximum likelihood?`,
      `<b>Method of moments</b>: set the model's first few theoretical moments equal to the sample moments and solve for the parameters; simple, but can be inefficient or give impossible values. <b>Maximum likelihood</b>: choose the parameters that make the observed data most probable under the model; asymptotically efficient and invariant to reparametrisation, but needs the full distribution and sometimes numerical optimisation. For many standard models (normal mean, Poisson rate) they coincide.`,
      [`One matches averages; the other maximises a probability.`, `Which uses more of the model's assumptions?`]),
    O('stationarity', 'Statistics', 'm', 'Stationarity', `What does stationarity mean for a time series?`,
      `<b>Weak (covariance) stationarity</b>: constant mean, finite constant variance, and autocovariance depending only on the lag, not on the date. (Strict stationarity: all joint distributions are shift-invariant.) Prices are usually non-stationary (random-walk-like); returns are closer to stationary. Stationarity matters because sample averages and fitted models only generalise if the process's properties don't drift.`,
      [`What should stay the same over time: the mean, the variance, the correlations?`, `Are stock prices stationary? Returns?`]),
    O('bsm-intuition', 'Options', 'm', 'The idea behind Black–Scholes', `What is the key idea behind the Black–Scholes–Merton model?`,
      `<b>Dynamic replication.</b> Hold the option and short Δ = ∂V/∂S shares; over an instant the random part of the stock's move cancels, so the hedged portfolio is riskless and must earn the risk-free rate. That gives the Black–Scholes PDE, in which investors' risk preferences and the stock's expected return don't appear, so the price is the risk-neutral expectation of the discounted payoff.`,
      [`What position in the stock removes the option's exposure to small moves?`, `What must a riskless portfolio earn?`]),
    O('delta-def', 'Options', 'e', 'Delta', `What is delta?`,
      `The sensitivity of the option's value to the underlying price, ∂V/∂S: how many shares the option behaves like. A call's delta lies between 0 and 1 (about ½ at the money in Black–Scholes, N(d₁)); a put's between −1 and 0. It's also the hedge ratio, and roughly (not exactly) the probability of finishing in the money.`,
      [`Which derivative of the price is it?`, `What range does it take for calls and puts?`]),
    O('no-arbitrage', 'Options', 'e', 'No-arbitrage pricing', `What is no-arbitrage pricing?`,
      `If a derivative's payoff can be replicated with traded assets, its price must equal the cost of the replicating portfolio; otherwise you could buy the cheaper and sell the dearer for a riskless profit. Equivalently, prices are discounted expectations under a risk-neutral measure. Forwards, put–call parity and Black–Scholes all come from this.`,
      [`What happens if two portfolios with the same payoff cost different amounts?`, `How does replication fix the price?`]),
    O('forward-derivation', 'Finance', 'e', 'Deriving the forward price', `Derive the forward price of a non-dividend stock.`,
      `F = S₀ e<sup>rT</sup>. If F were higher, borrow S₀, buy the stock, and sell the forward: at T deliver the stock for F and repay S₀e<sup>rT</sup>, a riskless profit. If F were lower, short the stock, invest the proceeds, and buy the forward. So only F = S₀e<sup>rT</sup> rules out arbitrage.`,
      [`Build a portfolio that ends up holding the stock at T without using a forward.`, `What does it cost to carry the stock until T?`]),
    ['forward-dividend', 'Finance', 'e', 'A forward with a dividend yield', `A stock index is at 100 with a continuous dividend yield of 2%; the risk-free rate is 5% (continuous).`,
      [{ q: `What is the 1-year forward price?`, a: 100 * Math.exp(0.03), tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Dividends you'd receive from holding the stock reduce the cost of carry.`, `F = S e<sup>(r − q)T</sup>.`],
        s: [`Cost of carry net of the dividend yield.`, [`100 e<sup>0.03</sup> ≈ <b>103.05</b>.`], `Without dividends it would be 105.13.`, ``] }]],
    O('parity-derivation', 'Options', 'm', 'Deriving put–call parity', `State and derive put–call parity for European options on a non-dividend stock.`,
      `C − P = S₀ − K e<sup>−rT</sup>. At expiry, a call plus K in cash (a bond paying K) is worth max(S, K); a put plus the stock is also worth max(S, K). Equal payoffs mean equal prices today: C + K e<sup>−rT</sup> = P + S₀. It holds whatever model you use, and doesn't hold for American options (where only bounds apply).`,
      [`Find two portfolios with the same payoff at expiry.`, `One uses the call and a bond; the other the put and the stock.`]),
    O('risk-neutral', 'Options', 'm', 'Risk-neutral pricing', `What is risk-neutral pricing, and does it assume investors are risk-neutral?`,
      `A derivative's price is the expected discounted payoff under a measure Q in which every traded asset's discounted price is a martingale (so all assets grow at the risk-free rate on average). It does <b>not</b> say investors are risk-neutral: Q is a pricing device implied by no-arbitrage, and real-world (P) expected returns and risk premia don't enter the price because the option can be hedged.`,
      [`Under which measure is the discounted stock price a martingale?`, `Why don't real-world expected returns matter for a hedgeable claim?`]),
    O('bsm-assumptions', 'Options', 'e', 'Black–Scholes assumptions', `What are the main assumptions of the Black–Scholes model?`,
      `The stock follows geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility; a constant risk-free rate; no dividends (in the basic version); continuous trading with no transaction costs or taxes; short selling and fractional shares allowed; no arbitrage. Real markets break several (vol isn't constant, there are jumps and costs), which is why we see a volatility smile.`,
      [`What does it assume about the stock's path and volatility?`, `And about trading?`]),
    ['bs-atm', 'Options', 'm', 'An at-the-money call', `Stock and strike are both 100, rates are zero, volatility is 20% and expiry is 1 year.`,
      [{ q: `What is the Black–Scholes call price?`, a: 100 * (2 * 0.539827837277029 - 1), tol: { abs: 0.02, rel: 0 },
        h: [`With r = 0 and S = K, d₁ = σ√T/2 = 0.1 and d₂ = −0.1.`, `C = S N(d₁) − K N(d₂) = 100(N(0.1) − N(−0.1)).`, `N(0.1) ≈ 0.5398. Also try the rule of thumb 0.4 σ S √T.`],
        s: [`At the money with zero rates, C = S(2N(σ√T/2) − 1).`, [`N(0.1) ≈ 0.5398, so C ≈ 100 × 0.0797 = <b>7.97</b>.`], `Rule of thumb 0.4 × 0.2 × 100 = 8.`, ``] }]],
    O('moneyness', 'Options', 'e', 'Moneyness', `What is moneyness?`,
      `How the strike compares with the underlying: in, at or out of the money. Measured as S/K, K/S, or better K/F or ln(K/F) relative to the forward, or by delta (a "25-delta put"). Measuring against the forward allows for rates and dividends, and delta-based moneyness makes options comparable across expiries.`,
      [`Compare the strike with what?`, `Why might the forward be a better reference than the spot?`]),
    ['time-value', 'Options', 'e', 'Intrinsic and time value', `A stock is at 105 and a call struck at 100 costs 8.`,
      [{ q: `How much of the price is time value?`, a: 3, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Intrinsic value of a call is max(S − K, 0).`, `Time value is the price minus the intrinsic value.`],
        s: [`Price = intrinsic + time value.`, [`Intrinsic = 5, so time value = 8 − 5 = <b>3</b>.`], `Time value is largest at the money and shrinks to zero at expiry.`, ``] }]],
    O('gamma-def', 'Options', 'e', 'Gamma', `What is gamma?`,
      `∂²V/∂S², the rate of change of delta: the option's convexity. Long options have positive gamma (your delta moves with the market, so delta-hedging buys low and sells high) and pay for it with theta. Gamma is largest at the money near expiry; high gamma means hedges need frequent rebalancing.`,
      [`Which derivative of the option price is it?`, `How does it relate to delta and to theta?`]),
    O('vega-def', 'Options', 'e', 'Vega', `What is vega?`,
      `∂V/∂σ, the change in option value per unit change in implied volatility (usually quoted per vol point). It's positive for long calls and puts, largest at the money and for longer expiries (∝ √T). ATM vega ≈ 0.4 S√T per 1.00 of vol, so about 0.004 S√T per point.`,
      [`Sensitivity to what input?`, `Where is it largest: short or long expiry, ATM or far OTM?`]),

    O('theta-def', 'Options', 'e', 'Theta', `What is theta?`,
      `∂V/∂t, the change in an option's value as time passes with everything else fixed (usually quoted per day). Long vanilla options normally have negative theta: their optionality decays. It's the price of gamma: in Black–Scholes, Θ + ½σ²S²Γ + rSΔ − rV = 0, so long gamma means paying theta. Deep in-the-money European puts can have positive theta.`,
      [`Hold everything fixed except the calendar.`, `What does a long option holder lose each day, and what do they get for it?`]),
    O('rho-def', 'Options', 'e', 'Rho', `What is rho?`,
      `∂V/∂r, sensitivity to the risk-free rate. Calls have positive rho (a higher rate lowers the present value of the strike you'll pay), puts negative. For a Black–Scholes call, ρ = K T e<sup>−rT</sup> N(d₂). It matters most for long-dated options.`,
      [`Which input does it measure sensitivity to?`, `Does a higher rate help the person who will pay the strike, or receive it?`]),
    ['bs-deltas', 'Options', 'm', 'Black–Scholes deltas', `S = K = 100, r = 0, σ = 20%, T = 1 year, no dividends.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the call's delta?`, a: 0.539827837277029, tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Call delta is N(d₁).`, `d₁ = (ln(S/K) + (r + σ²/2)T)/(σ√T) = 0.1.`],
        s: [`Δ<sub>call</sub> = N(d₁).`, [`d₁ = 0.02/0.2 = 0.1.`, `N(0.1) ≈ <b>0.540</b>.`], `Slightly above ½ at the money because of the σ²/2 term.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is the put's delta?`, a: 0.539827837277029 - 1, tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Put–call parity: Δ<sub>call</sub> − Δ<sub>put</sub> = 1.`, `So Δ<sub>put</sub> = N(d₁) − 1.`],
        s: [`Δ<sub>put</sub> = N(d₁) − 1.`, [`0.540 − 1 = <b>−0.460</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['bs-gamma', 'Options', 'm', 'Black–Scholes gamma', `S = K = 100, r = 0, σ = 20%, T = 1 year.`,
      [{ q: `What is the option's gamma (per $1 move in S)?`, a: Math.exp(-0.005) / Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI) / 20, tol: { abs: 0.0002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Γ = φ(d₁)/(Sσ√T), the same for the call and the put.`, `d₁ = 0.1 and φ(0.1) = e<sup>−0.005</sup>/√(2π) ≈ 0.397.`, `Sσ√T = 20.`],
        s: [`Γ = φ(d₁)/(Sσ√T).`, [`0.397/20 ≈ <b>0.0198</b>.`], `So a $1 rise lifts delta by about 0.02.`, ``] }]],
    ['bs-vega', 'Options', 'm', 'Black–Scholes vega', `S = K = 100, r = 0, σ = 20%, T = 1 year.`,
      [{ q: `By how much does the option's value change for a 1-point rise in volatility (20% → 21%)?`, a: 100 * Math.exp(-0.005) / Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI) * 0.01, tol: { abs: 0.003, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Vega = S φ(d₁) √T per 1.00 of volatility.`, `d₁ = 0.1, and φ(0.1) = e<sup>−0.005</sup>/√(2π).`, `A vol point is 0.01.`],
        s: [`Vega = Sφ(d₁)√T, then scale to one vol point.`, [`100 × 0.397 × 1 = 39.7 per 1.00 of vol.`, `Per point: <b>≈ 0.397</b>.`], `Rule of thumb 0.4 × S × √T / 100 = 0.4.`, ``] }]],
    O('greek-parity', 'Options', 'm', 'Greeks from put–call parity', `What relationships between call and put Greeks follow from put–call parity?`,
      `Differentiate C − P = S − K e<sup>−rT</sup>: Δ<sub>C</sub> − Δ<sub>P</sub> = 1; Γ<sub>C</sub> = Γ<sub>P</sub>; vega<sub>C</sub> = vega<sub>P</sub>; Θ<sub>C</sub> − Θ<sub>P</sub> = −rK e<sup>−rT</sup>; ρ<sub>C</sub> − ρ<sub>P</sub> = K T e<sup>−rT</sup>. Same strike and expiry, European options; with a dividend yield the delta relation becomes e<sup>−qT</sup>.`,
      [`Differentiate both sides of parity with respect to each input.`, `Which terms on the right depend on S, on σ, on t, on r?`]),
    O('implied-vol', 'Options', 'e', 'Implied volatility', `What is implied volatility?`,
      `The volatility that, put into Black–Scholes (or another model), reproduces an option's market price. It isn't observed or forecast directly; it's a way of quoting price. It reflects expected future volatility plus risk premia and supply and demand, and it differs by strike and expiry (the smile and term structure), which shows Black–Scholes is only an approximation.`,
      [`Which input to the pricing formula can't be observed?`, `Solve the formula backwards from the price.`]),
    O('smile-skew', 'Options', 'm', 'Smile and skew', `What are the volatility smile and skew?`,
      `When implied vols for the same expiry are plotted against strike, they aren't flat. A <b>smile</b> is higher at both wings (common in FX); a <b>skew</b> slopes down from low strikes to high (equity indices: downside puts are dearer). They reflect fat tails, jumps, the correlation between spot and vol, and demand for protection, none of which constant-vol Black–Scholes captures.`,
      [`Plot implied vol against strike for one expiry. What would Black–Scholes predict?`, `Why might low-strike puts be in demand?`]),
    ['digital-call', 'Options', 'm', 'A digital call', `A cash-or-nothing call pays $1 if S<sub>T</sub> > K. Take S = K = 100, r = 0, σ = 20%, T = 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is its Black–Scholes price?`, a: 1 - 0.539827837277029, tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Its price is e<sup>−rT</sup> times the risk-neutral probability of finishing in the money.`, `That probability is N(d₂).`, `d₂ = d₁ − σ√T = 0.1 − 0.2.`],
        s: [`Digital = e<sup>−rT</sup> N(d₂).`, [`d₂ = −0.1, N(−0.1) ≈ <b>0.460</b>.`], `Below ½: under the risk-neutral measure the median of S<sub>T</sub> is below the forward.`, `Using N(d₁) (the call's delta) instead of N(d₂).`] }]],
    O('hedge-pnl', 'Options', 'h', 'Delta hedging, gamma and theta', `How does delta hedging relate to gamma and theta?`,
      `Over a short interval, option P&L ≈ Δ dS + ½ Γ dS² + Θ dt. Delta hedging cancels the Δ dS term, leaving ½ Γ dS² + Θ dt. In Black–Scholes, Θ ≈ −½ Γ σ²<sub>implied</sub> S², so the hedged P&L ≈ ½ Γ S² (realised return² − σ²<sub>implied</sub> dt): long gamma wins when the stock moves more than the implied vol priced in, and pays theta when it doesn't.`,
      [`Expand the option value to second order in dS.`, `What's left once the first-order term is hedged?`, `Compare realised and implied variance.`]),
    O('replicating', 'Options', 'm', 'Replicating portfolios', `What is a replicating portfolio?`,
      `A self-financing mix of traded assets (usually the underlying and cash) whose value matches the derivative's payoff in every state. If one exists, the derivative's price must equal its cost, or there's an arbitrage. In a one-step binomial model you solve two equations for the number of shares and the bond; in Black–Scholes you hold Δ shares and rebalance continuously.`,
      [`Match the payoff state by state using the stock and a bond.`, `What must the derivative cost if you can do that?`]),
    O('american-value', 'Options', 'm', 'American v European', `Why can an American option be worth more than a European one?`,
      `It has every right the European option has plus the right to exercise early, so it can't be worth less. The extra is worth something when early exercise is sometimes optimal: deep in-the-money puts (getting K now earns interest), or calls just before a dividend. For calls on non-dividend stocks, early exercise is never optimal, so the American and European calls are worth the same.`,
      [`Can an extra right ever reduce value?`, `When would you want your money (or the stock) now rather than later?`]),
    ['put-bound', 'Options', 'm', 'A lower bound on a put', `S = 100, K = 110, r = 5% (continuous), T = 1, no dividends.`,
      [{ q: `What is the lowest no-arbitrage price for a European put?`, a: 110 * Math.exp(-0.05) - 100, tol: { abs: 0.02, rel: 0 },
        h: [`A put plus the stock is worth at least K at expiry.`, `So P + S ≥ K e<sup>−rT</sup>.`],
        s: [`P ≥ max(K e<sup>−rT</sup> − S, 0).`, [`110 e<sup>−0.05</sup> ≈ 104.64.`, `P ≥ 104.64 − 100 = <b>4.64</b>.`], `Note it's below the intrinsic value 10: a European put can trade under intrinsic.`, ``] }]],
    O('completeness', 'Options', 'h', 'Market completeness', `What does it mean for a market to be complete?`,
      `Every payoff can be replicated by trading the available assets. In a complete, arbitrage-free market the risk-neutral measure is unique, so every derivative has one price (Black–Scholes and the binomial model are complete). With jumps or stochastic volatility and too few traded instruments the market is incomplete: many risk-neutral measures fit, and prices depend on extra choices (calibration to other options, risk preferences).`,
      [`Can every payoff be built from traded assets?`, `How many risk-neutral measures are there then?`]),
    O('implied-realised', 'Options', 'm', 'Implied v realised volatility', `What's the difference between implied and realised volatility?`,
      `Implied volatility is backed out of option prices and is forward-looking under the pricing measure. Realised volatility is measured from actual returns over a past window. On average implied has exceeded subsequent realised (the volatility risk premium), which is why selling options earns money most of the time and loses heavily occasionally. A delta-hedged option's P&L depends on realised versus implied.`,
      [`One comes from option prices, the other from price history.`, `Which is usually bigger, and why might that be?`]),
    O('dividends-forward', 'Options', 'm', 'Dividends in pricing', `How do you incorporate dividends into option pricing?`,
      `With a continuous yield q, replace S by S e<sup>−qT</sup>: the forward is F = S e<sup>(r−q)T</sup>, and in Black–Scholes d₁ = (ln(S/K) + (r − q + σ²/2)T)/(σ√T), C = S e<sup>−qT</sup>N(d₁) − K e<sup>−rT</sup>N(d₂). Discrete known dividends: subtract their present value from S. Higher dividends lower calls and raise puts.`,
      [`What does a dividend do to the forward price?`, `Where does q go in the Black–Scholes formula?`]),
    O('vol-surface', 'Options', 'h', 'The implied volatility surface', `What is an implied volatility surface?`,
      `Implied vol as a function of strike (or moneyness/delta) and expiry. It summarises the whole option market in one object: the skew across strikes and the term structure across expiries. Traders interpolate it to price any option, risk-manage against it, and must keep it free of arbitrage (no calendar arbitrage between expiries, no butterfly arbitrage across strikes).`,
      [`Two dimensions: what are the axes?`, `What constraints stop the surface admitting arbitrage?`]),
    O('risk-reversal', 'Options', 'm', 'Risk reversals', `What is a risk reversal?`,
      `Long an out-of-the-money call and short an out-of-the-money put (often both 25-delta), or the reverse. As a quote it's the difference in implied vol between the call and the put, a measure of skew: in equity indices it's usually negative (downside puts dearer). As a trade it's a cheap bullish (or bearish) position with skew exposure.`,
      [`Which two options, and which way round?`, `What does the vol difference between them tell you?`]),
    ['butterfly', 'Options', 'e', 'A butterfly spread', `Buy a 90 call, sell two 100 calls and buy a 110 call, all with the same expiry.`,
      [{ q: `What is the largest payoff at expiry?`, a: 10, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Work out the payoff with the stock at 90, 100 and 110.`, `The payoff is a tent peaking at the middle strike.`],
        s: [`The tent peaks at the middle strike.`, [`At S = 100: (100 − 90) − 0 + 0 = <b>10</b>.`], `It's zero below 90 and above 110, so the butterfly is a bet on the stock finishing near 100.`, ``] }]],
    ['straddle', 'Options', 'e', 'Straddle break-even', `A 100-strike call costs 5 and the 100-strike put costs 3 (same expiry). You buy both.`,
      [{ q: `Above what stock price at expiry do you make money on the upside?`, a: 108, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`The straddle cost 8 in total.`, `On the upside only the call pays.`],
        s: [`Break-evens are the strike ± the total premium.`, [`100 + 8 = <b>108</b> (and 92 on the downside).`], `A long straddle is long gamma and vega and pays theta.`, ``] }]],
    O('model-risk', 'Options', 'm', 'Model risk', `What is model risk in derivatives pricing?`,
      `The risk that prices or hedges are wrong because the model's assumptions don't hold (constant vol, no jumps, lognormal prices), its parameters are badly estimated or calibrated, or it's implemented wrongly. Examples: pricing exotics off a flat vol, interpolating the smile badly, hedging with model deltas that ignore spot–vol correlation. Managed with model validation, reserves, stress tests and comparing models.`,
      [`Three things can go wrong: the assumptions, the inputs, and the code.`, `Give an option-market example of each.`]),
    ['binomial-step', 'Options', 'm', 'A one-step binomial tree', `A stock at 100 moves to 120 or 90 in one period; the gross risk-free return is 1.05.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the risk-neutral probability of the up move?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Under Q the stock must grow at the risk-free rate: p × 120 + (1 − p) × 90 = 105.`, `So p = (R − d)/(u − d).`],
        s: [`Risk-neutral probability makes the stock earn R.`, [`(1.05 − 0.9)/(1.2 − 0.9) = <b>0.5</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is a call struck at 100 worth?`, a: 10 / 1.05, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`The call pays 20 after an up move and 0 after a down move.`, `Price = discounted risk-neutral expectation.`],
        s: [`Discount the risk-neutral expected payoff.`, [`(0.5 × 20 + 0.5 × 0)/1.05 = <b>9.52</b>.`], `Replication check: hold 2/3 of a share and borrow 57.14.`, ``] }]],
    O('binomial-intuition', 'Options', 'm', 'The binomial model', `What is the intuition behind the binomial option-pricing model?`,
      `Over each small step the stock goes up or down. At expiry the payoffs are known; working backwards, each node's value is the discounted risk-neutral average of the two values after it (and, for American options, the larger of that and exercising now). Each step can be replicated with the stock and a bond, so this is no-arbitrage pricing; as the steps shrink it converges to Black–Scholes.`,
      [`Start at expiry, where you know the payoff.`, `How do you value a node from the two nodes after it?`]),
    O('forward-futures', 'Finance', 'e', 'Forwards v futures', `What's the difference between a forward and a futures contract?`,
      `A forward is an over-the-counter, customised contract settled at maturity, with counterparty credit risk. A future is exchange-traded, standardised, cleared, margined and marked to market daily. Daily settlement means futures and forward prices differ slightly when interest rates are random and correlated with the underlying (the convexity adjustment).`,
      [`Where is each traded, and how is it settled?`, `Why could daily settlement change the fair price?`]),
    O('payoffs', 'Options', 'e', 'Call and put payoffs', `What are the payoffs of European calls and puts?`,
      `Call: max(S<sub>T</sub> − K, 0). Put: max(K − S<sub>T</sub>, 0). These are payoffs at expiry, before subtracting the premium paid. A long call has unlimited upside and loses at most its premium; a short call has unlimited downside. Call minus put with the same strike pays S<sub>T</sub> − K, a forward.`,
      [`When does each pay off, and how much?`, `What does a call minus a put pay?`]),
    O('breeden-litzenberger', 'Options', 'h', 'Prices and the risk-neutral density', `How are option prices related to the risk-neutral density?`,
      `Breeden–Litzenberger: ∂²C/∂K² = e<sup>−rT</sup> f<sub>Q</sub>(K). The second derivative of call prices with respect to strike is the discounted risk-neutral density of S<sub>T</sub>. A butterfly approximates this derivative, and its price is the (discounted) probability of finishing near the middle strike. It's how you read the market's implied distribution off the smile.`,
      [`Differentiate C(K) = e<sup>−rT</sup> ∫ (s − K)⁺ f(s) ds twice with respect to K.`, `Which option strategy is a discrete second difference in strike?`]),

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- stochastic calculus
    O('brownian-def', 'Stochastic calculus', 'e', 'Brownian motion', `What is standard Brownian motion?`,
      `A process W<sub>t</sub> with W₀ = 0, continuous paths, and independent, stationary, normally distributed increments: W<sub>t</sub> − W<sub>s</sub> ~ N(0, t − s). Its paths are continuous but nowhere differentiable and have quadratic variation t. It's the limit of a scaled random walk.`,
      [`Four properties: the start, the paths, and two about increments.`, `What is the distribution of W<sub>t</sub> − W<sub>s</sub>?`]),
    ['bm-moments', 'Stochastic calculus', 'e', 'Brownian moments', `W is standard Brownian motion.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is Var(W₅ − W₂)?`, a: 3, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Increments are normal with variance equal to the elapsed time.`],
        s: [`W<sub>t</sub> − W<sub>s</sub> ~ N(0, t − s).`, [`5 − 2 = <b>3</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is E[W₂ W₅]?`, a: 2, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Write W₅ = W₂ + (W₅ − W₂), an independent increment.`, `E[W₂²] = 2 and the cross term has mean zero.`],
        s: [`Cov(W<sub>s</sub>, W<sub>t</sub>) = min(s, t).`, [`E[W₂²] + E[W₂(W₅ − W₂)] = 2 + 0 = <b>2</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['quadratic-variation', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Quadratic variation', `W is standard Brownian motion.`,
      [{ q: `What is the quadratic variation of W over [0, 4]?`, a: 4, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Sum the squared increments over a fine partition.`, `Each squared increment averages its time step, and the fluctuations average out.`],
        s: [`[W]<sub>T</sub> = T (written dW² = dt).`, [`Over [0, 4]: <b>4</b>.`], `A smooth function has quadratic variation 0; Brownian paths are far rougher.`, ``] }]],
    O('ito-rules', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Itô multiplication rules', `What are the basic Itô multiplication rules?`,
      `dt · dt = 0, dt · dW = 0, dW · dW = dt (and for two Brownian motions with correlation ρ, dW¹ dW² = ρ dt). The surprising one, dW² = dt, is quadratic variation: squared Brownian increments don't vanish at first order. It's why Itô's lemma has an extra ½σ² f<sub>xx</sub> term.`,
      [`Which products are of smaller order than dt and drop out?`, `What is the size of dW?`]),
    O('ito-lemma', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', "Itô's lemma", `State Itô's lemma for a one-dimensional Itô process.`,
      `If dX = μ dt + σ dW and f(t, x) is smooth, then df = (f<sub>t</sub> + μ f<sub>x</sub> + ½ σ² f<sub>xx</sub>) dt + σ f<sub>x</sub> dW. It's the chain rule with a second-order term, because (dX)² = σ² dt doesn't vanish. Example: for f = ln S with dS = μS dt + σS dW, d ln S = (μ − σ²/2) dt + σ dW.`,
      [`Do a Taylor expansion to second order.`, `Keep the (dX)² term and use dW² = dt.`]),
    ['gbm-median', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Mean and median of GBM', `A stock follows dS = μS dt + σS dW with S₀ = 100, μ = 10% and σ = 30%.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is E[S₁]?`, a: 100 * Math.exp(0.1), tol: { abs: 0.02, rel: 0 },
        h: [`The drift μ is the expected growth rate of S itself.`, `E[S<sub>t</sub>] = S₀ e<sup>μt</sup>.`],
        s: [`The mean grows at μ.`, [`100 e<sup>0.1</sup> ≈ <b>110.52</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is the median of S₁?`, a: 100 * Math.exp(0.1 - 0.045), tol: { abs: 0.02, rel: 0 },
        h: [`S₁ = S₀ exp((μ − σ²/2) + σW₁), and W₁ has median 0.`, `σ²/2 = 0.045.`],
        s: [`The median grows at μ − σ²/2 (volatility drag).`, [`100 e<sup>0.055</sup> ≈ <b>105.65</b>.`], `Mean above median: the lognormal is right-skewed.`, `Using μ for the median as well.`] }]],
    O('gbm-why', 'Stochastic calculus', 'e', 'Why GBM for stocks', `Why is geometric Brownian motion used in Black–Scholes?`,
      `It models percentage (not absolute) moves, so prices stay positive and volatility scales with price; log returns are normal and independent over time; and it's simple enough to give closed-form option prices. Its weaknesses (constant vol, no jumps, thin tails) are what smiles and stochastic-vol models address.`,
      [`What should happen to the size of moves as the price rises?`, `Can a GBM price go negative?`]),
    O('martingale', 'Stochastic calculus', 'e', 'Martingales', `What is a martingale?`,
      `A process M<sub>t</sub> with E|M<sub>t</sub>| < ∞ and E[M<sub>t</sub> | F<sub>s</sub>] = M<sub>s</sub> for s ≤ t: given everything known now, the best forecast of any future value is the current value. A fair game. Examples: a symmetric random walk, Brownian motion, W<sub>t</sub>² − t, and discounted asset prices under the risk-neutral measure.`,
      [`What is the expected future value given what you know now?`, `Name a few examples, including one built from W².`]),
    O('discounted-martingale', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Discounted prices as martingales', `Why are discounted asset prices martingales under the risk-neutral measure?`,
      `That's what defines Q: a measure equivalent to P under which every traded asset earns the risk-free rate on average, so e<sup>−rt</sup>S<sub>t</sub> has no drift. The fundamental theorem of asset pricing says such a measure exists exactly when there's no arbitrage. Then any replicable claim's discounted value is a martingale too, so its price is e<sup>−rT</sup>E<sub>Q</sub>[payoff].`,
      [`What expected return does each asset have under Q?`, `What links "no arbitrage" and "a martingale measure exists"?`]),
    O('girsanov', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', "Girsanov's theorem", `What is the intuition behind Girsanov's theorem?`,
      `Changing probability measure (reweighting paths) changes a Brownian motion's <b>drift</b> but not its volatility. Under the new measure, W̃<sub>t</sub> = W<sub>t</sub> + θt is a Brownian motion, with the weights given by an exponential martingale. In Black–Scholes, with θ = (μ − r)/σ, the stock's drift changes from μ to r while σ stays the same, which is why volatility matters for prices and μ doesn't.`,
      [`Reweighting paths can tilt them up or down. Can it change how wiggly they are?`, `What happens to the stock's drift when you go from P to Q?`]),
    ['market-price-risk', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'The market price of risk', `In Black–Scholes, a stock has expected return 10%, volatility 20%, and the risk-free rate is 3%.`,
      [{ q: `What is its market price of risk?`, a: 0.35,
        h: [`It's the excess return per unit of volatility.`, `λ = (μ − r)/σ.`],
        s: [`λ = (μ − r)/σ, the Sharpe ratio of the stock.`, [`(0.10 − 0.03)/0.20 = <b>0.35</b>.`], `It's the drift shift Girsanov removes to reach the risk-neutral measure.`, ``] }]],
    O('ito-integral', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'The Itô integral', `What is the key idea behind the Itô integral?`,
      `∫ H dW is the limit of sums Σ H(t<sub>i</sub>)(W(t<sub>i+1</sub>) − W(t<sub>i</sub>)) with the integrand evaluated at the <b>left</b> end of each interval, so H can't peek at the move it multiplies. That makes it the gains from a trading strategy that decides holdings before the price moves, and makes the integral a martingale with mean 0.`,
      [`Where in each interval is the integrand evaluated?`, `Why is that the right choice for trading gains?`]),
    ['ito-isometry', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Itô isometry', `W is standard Brownian motion.`,
      [{ q: `What is E[(∫₀¹ t dW<sub>t</sub>)²]?`, a: 1 / 3,
        h: [`Itô isometry: E[(∫ H dW)²] = E[∫ H² dt].`, `Here H = t is deterministic.`],
        s: [`Isometry turns the second moment into an ordinary integral.`, [`∫₀¹ t² dt = <b>1/3</b>.`], `∫₀¹ t dW is normal with mean 0 and variance 1/3.`, ``] }]],
    O('feynman-kac', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Feynman–Kac', `What does the Feynman–Kac theorem say in option pricing?`,
      `The solution of a parabolic PDE such as the Black–Scholes equation V<sub>t</sub> + rSV<sub>S</sub> + ½σ²S²V<sub>SS</sub> − rV = 0 with terminal value V(T, S) = payoff can be written as an expectation: V(t, S) = E<sub>Q</sub>[e<sup>−r(T−t)</sup> payoff | S<sub>t</sub> = S], where under Q the stock grows at r. It links the PDE (hedging) view and the risk-neutral expectation (Monte Carlo) view.`,
      [`One side is a PDE with a terminal condition; the other is an expectation.`, `Under which dynamics is the expectation taken?`]),
    O('optional-stopping', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Optional stopping', `What is the optional stopping theorem, and when can it fail?`,
      `For a martingale M and a stopping time τ, E[M<sub>τ</sub>] = M₀, provided something keeps things bounded: τ bounded, or M stopped at τ bounded, or E[τ] < ∞ with bounded increments. It fails without such conditions: "bet on a fair coin until you're $1 ahead" stops with probability 1 at +1, so E[M<sub>τ</sub>] = 1 ≠ 0, because E[τ] is infinite and losses along the way are unbounded.`,
      [`Can you beat a fair game by choosing when to stop?`, `Look at "stop when $1 ahead": what goes wrong?`]),
    O('filtration', 'Stochastic calculus', 'e', 'Filtrations and adapted processes', `What is a filtration, and what does it mean for a process to be adapted?`,
      `A filtration (F<sub>t</sub>) is an increasing family of σ-algebras: the information available at each time, never forgotten. A process is adapted if its value at time t is known from F<sub>t</sub>. Trading strategies must be adapted (you can only act on what you know), which is why the Itô integral uses left-endpoint integrands.`,
      [`Information grows over time; how do you model that?`, `What does it mean for a quantity to be "known at time t"?`]),
    O('ito-stratonovich', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Itô v Stratonovich', `What's the difference between Itô and Stratonovich integrals?`,
      `Itô evaluates the integrand at the left end of each interval; Stratonovich at the midpoint. Stratonovich keeps the ordinary chain rule (no ½σ² f<sub>xx</sub> term) and is natural in physics, but its integrals aren't martingales and the integrand "looks ahead". Finance uses Itô because a trading strategy must be chosen before the price moves. The two differ by a drift correction ½ ∫ dH dW.`,
      [`Where is the integrand evaluated in each?`, `Which keeps the ordinary chain rule, and which matches trading?`]),
    ['ou-process', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'An Ornstein–Uhlenbeck spread', `A spread follows dX = κ(θ − X) dt + σ dW with κ = 0.5 per year and σ = 0.2.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the half-life of a deviation from θ, in years?`, a: Math.log(2) / 0.5, tol: { abs: 0.005, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Without noise, the deviation decays like e<sup>−κt</sup>.`, `Solve e<sup>−κt</sup> = ½.`],
        s: [`Half-life = ln 2 / κ.`, [`0.693/0.5 ≈ <b>1.39</b> years.`], `A long half-life makes mean reversion slow to trade.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is the long-run variance of X?`, a: 0.04,
        h: [`The stationary distribution is normal around θ.`, `Its variance is σ²/(2κ).`],
        s: [`Stationary variance σ²/(2κ).`, [`0.04/1 = <b>0.04</b> (standard deviation 0.2).`], ``, ``] }]],
    O('hitting-times', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Hitting times', `What is a hitting time, and where does it appear in finance?`,
      `τ<sub>a</sub> = inf{t ≥ 0 : X<sub>t</sub> = a}, the first time a process reaches level a. For Brownian motion, the reflection principle gives P(τ<sub>a</sub> ≤ t) = 2P(W<sub>t</sub> ≥ a). Hitting times are stopping times and appear in barrier options, stop-losses, default (structural credit models) and optimal exercise.`,
      [`Define it as an infimum.`, `What does the reflection principle say about Brownian motion?`]),
    O('barrier-stopping', 'Options', 'm', 'Barriers and stopping times', `Why are barrier options linked to stopping times?`,
      `A barrier option is knocked in or out the first time the underlying crosses a level, a hitting time. It's a stopping time because whether it has happened by t depends only on the path up to t. That's why barrier prices use reflection-principle formulas, and why discrete monitoring (checking only at fixing times) changes the price.`,
      [`When does the barrier "happen", and what information decides it?`, `What changes if the barrier is only checked daily?`]),
    O('local-martingale', 'Stochastic calculus', 'h', 'Local martingales', `What's the difference between a martingale and a local martingale?`,
      `A local martingale becomes a martingale when stopped at a sequence of stopping times increasing to infinity, but may fail to be one itself: its expectation can drift (for example downwards, for a positive strict local martingale). Every martingale is a local martingale. Stochastic integrals ∫ H dW are local martingales, and true martingales only with enough integrability; the gap matters for bubbles and doubling strategies.`,
      [`Stop the process early enough and it's a martingale. Does that make it one overall?`, `Where does integrability come in?`]),
    O('stoch-vol', 'Options', 'm', 'Stochastic volatility', `Why go beyond Black–Scholes to stochastic volatility models?`,
      `Black–Scholes' constant volatility can't fit the observed smile and term structure, or volatility clustering. Letting volatility follow its own random process (Heston, SABR), correlated with the stock, produces skew (negative spot–vol correlation), smiles (vol of vol), and realistic dynamics for hedging and pricing exotics. The cost: extra parameters, incompleteness, calibration.`,
      [`What does the market's implied vol surface show that constant vol can't?`, `What does correlation between spot and vol produce?`]),
    O('jumps', 'Options', 'm', 'Adding jumps', `Why add jumps to diffusion models?`,
      `Diffusions move continuously, so they make large overnight or crash moves extremely unlikely and give too little short-dated skew. Adding Poisson-driven jumps (Merton, Kou) captures earnings gaps and crashes, fatter tails, and steep short-expiry skews. Jumps also make the market incomplete: they can't be hedged perfectly with the stock alone.`,
      [`Can a continuous path produce a gap?`, `Which part of the vol surface do jumps affect most?`]),
    O('self-financing', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Self-financing strategies', `What is a self-financing strategy?`,
      `A trading strategy with no money added or withdrawn after the start: any purchase is paid for by sales or borrowing within the portfolio, so the change in value comes only from price moves: dV = Δ dS + r(V − ΔS) dt. Replication arguments need this, otherwise you could "match" a payoff by injecting cash.`,
      [`Where does the money for each trade come from?`, `Write the change in value using only gains on holdings.`]),
    O('bs-pde', 'Options', 'm', 'From Itô to the Black–Scholes PDE', `How does Itô's lemma lead to the Black–Scholes PDE?`,
      `Itô gives dV = (V<sub>t</sub> + μSV<sub>S</sub> + ½σ²S²V<sub>SS</sub>) dt + σSV<sub>S</sub> dW. Hold the option and short V<sub>S</sub> shares: the dW terms cancel, so the portfolio is riskless and must earn r: V<sub>t</sub> + ½σ²S²V<sub>SS</sub> = r(V − SV<sub>S</sub>). Rearranged, V<sub>t</sub> + rSV<sub>S</sub> + ½σ²S²V<sub>SS</sub> − rV = 0, with μ gone.`,
      [`Apply Itô to V(t, S).`, `Choose a share holding that removes the dW term.`, `What must a riskless portfolio earn?`]),
    ['log-returns', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Log returns under GBM', `A stock follows GBM with μ = 8% and σ = 20%.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the mean of the one-year log return ln(S₁/S₀)?`, a: 0.06,
        h: [`By Itô, d ln S = (μ − σ²/2) dt + σ dW.`, `σ²/2 = 0.02.`],
        s: [`Log returns are normal with mean (μ − σ²/2)T.`, [`0.08 − 0.02 = <b>0.06</b>.`], ``, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is its standard deviation?`, a: 0.2,
        h: [`The dW term is σ dW, with variance σ²T.`],
        s: [`SD = σ√T.`, [`<b>0.2</b>.`], ``, ``] }]],
    ['vol-scaling', 'Statistics', 'e', 'Scaling volatility', `A stock's daily returns are independent with volatility 1%. There are 252 trading days a year.`,
      [{ q: `What is its annualised volatility, in percent?`, a: Math.sqrt(252), tol: { abs: 0.05, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Variances of independent returns add.`, `Annual variance = 252 × daily variance, so volatility scales with √252.`],
        s: [`σ<sub>annual</sub> = σ<sub>daily</sub> √252.`, [`1% × √252 ≈ <b>15.9%</b>.`], `Rule of thumb: daily vol × 16.`, `Multiplying by 252.`] }]],
    O('brownian-scaling', 'Stochastic calculus', 'm', 'Brownian scaling', `What is Brownian scaling?`,
      `For c > 0, W<sub>ct</sub> has the same distribution (as a process) as √c W<sub>t</sub>. Speeding time up by c scales the size of moves by √c, because variance grows linearly in time. That's why volatility scales with √T, and why Brownian paths look equally rough at every zoom.`,
      [`How does the variance of W<sub>t</sub> grow with t?`, `If time runs c times faster, how much bigger are the moves?`]),

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- coding, part 1
    O('code-returns', 'Coding', 'e', 'Simple and log returns', `Write a Python function that turns a price array into simple and log returns.`,
      `<pre><code>import numpy as np
def returns(prices):
    p = np.asarray(prices, dtype=float)
    if p.ndim != 1 or len(p) &lt; 2 or (p &lt;= 0).any():
        raise ValueError("need at least two positive prices")
    simple = p[1:] / p[:-1] - 1
    log = np.diff(np.log(p))
    return simple, log</code></pre><p>Log returns add over time (sum of log returns = log of the total); simple returns aggregate across assets in a portfolio. They're close for small moves: log(1 + r) ≈ r − r²/2.</p>`,
      [`Simple: P<sub>t</sub>/P<sub>t−1</sub> − 1. Log: ln(P<sub>t</sub>/P<sub>t−1</sub>).`, `Vectorise with NumPy slicing or np.diff, and validate the input.`]),
    O('code-rolling-vol', 'Coding', 'e', 'Rolling volatility', `Write a Python function for annualised rolling volatility from daily returns.`,
      `<pre><code>import pandas as pd, numpy as np
def rolling_vol(returns, window=21, periods=252):
    r = pd.Series(returns, dtype=float)
    return r.rolling(window).std(ddof=1) * np.sqrt(periods)</code></pre><p>Use the sample standard deviation over each window and scale by √(periods per year). The first window − 1 values are missing. Alternatives: an exponentially weighted std (<code>r.ewm(span=...).std()</code>), which reacts faster.</p>`,
      [`Take a standard deviation over a moving window.`, `How do you annualise a daily volatility?`]),
    O('code-bs-vector', 'Coding', 'm', 'Vectorised Black–Scholes', `Write a vectorised Python function for the Black–Scholes call price.`,
      `<pre><code>import numpy as np
from scipy.stats import norm
def bs_call(S, K, T, r, sigma):
    S, K, T, sigma = map(np.asarray, (S, K, T, sigma))
    if (T &lt;= 0).any() or (sigma &lt;= 0).any():
        raise ValueError("T and sigma must be positive")
    sq = sigma * np.sqrt(T)
    d1 = (np.log(S / K) + (r + 0.5 * sigma**2) * T) / sq
    d2 = d1 - sq
    return S * norm.cdf(d1) - K * np.exp(-r * T) * norm.cdf(d2)</code></pre><p>NumPy broadcasting prices whole grids at once. Edge cases: T → 0 (return intrinsic value) and σ → 0 (discounted forward intrinsic).</p>`,
      [`Compute d₁ and d₂ with NumPy so arrays broadcast.`, `Use scipy.stats.norm.cdf for N(·), and think about T = 0.`]),
    O('code-implied-vol', 'Coding', 'm', 'Implied volatility by bisection', `Write a Python function that finds implied volatility by bisection.`,
      `<pre><code>def implied_vol(price, S, K, T, r, lo=1e-6, hi=5.0, tol=1e-8):
    f = lambda s: bs_call(S, K, T, r, s) - price
    if f(lo) &gt; 0 or f(hi) &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("price outside no-arbitrage bounds")
    while hi - lo &gt; tol:
        mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)
        if f(mid) &gt; 0: hi = mid
        else: lo = mid
    return 0.5 * (lo + hi)</code></pre><p>It works because the call price increases with σ (vega > 0). Bisection is slow but always converges; Newton's method (using vega) is much faster near the money but can fail far from it.</p>`,
      [`The call price is monotonic in σ, so a root is bracketed.`, `Halve the interval until it's small enough. Why check the bounds first?`]),
    O('code-mc-call', 'Coding', 'm', 'Monte Carlo call price', `Write a Monte Carlo estimator for a European call.`,
      `<pre><code>import numpy as np
def mc_call(S, K, T, r, sigma, q=0.0, n=1_000_000, seed=0):
    z = np.random.default_rng(seed).standard_normal(n)
    ST = S * np.exp((r - q - 0.5 * sigma**2) * T + sigma * np.sqrt(T) * z)
    pay = np.exp(-r * T) * np.maximum(ST - K, 0)
    return pay.mean(), pay.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)</code></pre><p>Simulate S<sub>T</sub> under the risk-neutral measure, average the discounted payoff, and report the standard error, which falls like 1/√n. Compare with the closed form as a check.</p>`,
      [`Under Q, ln S<sub>T</sub> is normal with mean ln S + (r − q − σ²/2)T.`, `Average the discounted payoff, and report a standard error.`]),
    O('antithetic', 'Coding', 'm', 'Antithetic variates', `How do antithetic variates reduce Monte Carlo variance?`,
      `For each normal draw Z also use −Z, and average the two payoffs. The pair has the same mean, and when the payoff is monotonic in Z (a vanilla call) the two are negatively correlated, so their average has lower variance than two independent draws. It's free to implement and never biases the estimate; the gain is small for payoffs that aren't monotonic (e.g. a straddle).`,
      [`Pair each draw with its mirror image.`, `When are the two payoffs negatively correlated?`]),
    O('code-binomial', 'Coding', 'm', 'A binomial tree pricer', `Write a binomial-tree pricer for a European call.`,
      `<pre><code>import numpy as np
def binomial_call(S, K, T, r, sigma, n=500):
    dt = T / n
    u = np.exp(sigma * np.sqrt(dt)); d = 1 / u
    p = (np.exp(r * dt) - d) / (u - d)
    j = np.arange(n + 1)
    V = np.maximum(S * u**j * d**(n - j) - K, 0)
    disc = np.exp(-r * dt)
    for _ in range(n):
        V = disc * (p * V[1:] + (1 - p) * V[:-1])
    return V[0]</code></pre><p>Cox–Ross–Rubinstein: up and down factors e<sup>±σ√dt</sup>, risk-neutral p, then backward induction. It converges to Black–Scholes as n grows (oscillating; averaging n and n + 1 helps).</p>`,
      [`Pick u, d and the risk-neutral p for each step.`, `Start from the terminal payoffs and step back one level at a time.`]),
    O('code-american-put', 'Coding', 'h', 'An American put on a tree', `How do you change a binomial tree to price an American put?`,
      `At every node during the backward pass take the larger of continuing and exercising: <code>V = np.maximum(disc * (p * V[1:] + (1 - p) * V[:-1]), K - S_nodes)</code>, where <code>S_nodes = S * u**j * d**(level - j)</code> for that level. Exercise is optimal deep in the money, where receiving K now earns interest. The early-exercise boundary is where the two values meet.`,
      [`At each node you may exercise or continue.`, `Compute the stock price at every node of the level you're on.`]),
    O('code-hist-var', 'Coding', 'm', 'Historical VaR', `Write a function for historical value at risk.`,
      `<pre><code>import numpy as np
def historical_var(returns, level=0.95, value=1.0):
    r = np.asarray(returns, dtype=float)
    return -np.quantile(r, 1 - level) * value</code></pre><p>Sort past returns and take the (1 − level) quantile; VaR is minus that times the position value. It makes no distribution assumption but needs a long window, ignores scenarios not in the data, and says nothing about losses beyond the quantile (use expected shortfall for that).</p>`,
      [`Which quantile of past returns marks the worst 5%?`, `Express it as a positive loss on the position.`]),
  ]);
})();
